3. Platform and Technical Architecture

3.1 Required development workflow

The entire development process must accommodate the following workflow:

1. Google AI Studio generates and refines the source code.
2. GitHub stores the complete project and version history.
3. Codemagic builds the Android application in the cloud.
4. An APK is installed on a physical Android device for testing.
5. Bugs and performance issues are fixed through incremental development.

No requirement should assume a powerful local computer or a paid cloud build service.

3.2 Recommended technology stack

Layer| Technology| Responsibility
Native application| Kotlin| Android integration and application lifecycle
Native interface| Jetpack Compose| Navigation, settings, project management and native screens
3D simulation| Godot Engine 4.x| 3D scene, camera, models, physics and interactive simulation
Android/3D bridge| Godot Android library or supported embedding integration| Communication between the native shell and simulator
Simulation logic| GDScript and Godot physics APIs| Robot control, sensors, simulation and experiment logic
Project storage| Room + versioned JSON project files| Metadata, robot designs and experiment persistence
Preferences| DataStore| Theme, editor settings and user preferences
Local assets| Bundled resources and downloadable optional asset packs| Robot models, materials and environments
Build system| Gradle and Codemagic| Compilation, testing and APK generation
Source control| GitHub| Version control and reproducible builds

Architecture constraint: Do not assume that a standard Kotlin Android project can directly run an arbitrary Godot scene. Verify the supported Godot Android embedding approach and Gradle integration first.

If embedding creates an unacceptable technical or build obstacle, use a standalone Godot Android application with a fully designed native-feeling interface. Preserve the product requirements and document the trade-off instead of shipping a broken hybrid application.

The chosen architecture must be validated with a minimal working prototype before implementing the full feature set.

3.3 Zero-budget requirements

- No paid AI API is required for core functionality.
- No mandatory backend or account system.
- No mandatory cloud database.
- No paid 3D asset dependency.
- Use original procedural assets, properly licensed free assets, and self-created models.
- No mandatory analytics service.
- Offline access to saved projects and core simulations.
- Optional cloud synchronization must not be required for MVP functionality.

Third-party licenses must be respected and documented.

