6. Robot Workshop

The workshop is the core design environment.

6.1 Three-dimensional workspace

Provide an interactive 3D viewport supporting:

- Orbit camera controls.
- Pinch-to-zoom.
- Pan.
- Perspective and orthographic views.
- Grid and axis visualization.
- Ground plane and adjustable environment lighting.
- Component selection.
- Selection highlighting.
- Translation and rotation gizmos.
- Snap-to-grid placement.
- Undo and redo.
- Camera reset.
- Frame selected component.

Touch controls must be designed for phones rather than copied directly from desktop CAD software.

Users should be able to manipulate objects without precision mouse input.

6.2 Component library

Provide categorized components.

Structural components

- Chassis and base plates.
- Frames and brackets.
- Rigid links.
- Mounting plates.
- Wheels and casters.
- Generic primitives for custom structures.

Motion components

- DC motor.
- Servo motor.
- Revolute joint.
- Prismatic joint.
- Fixed joint.
- Wheel drive assembly.
- Generic articulated arm links.

Sensor components

- Distance sensor.
- Ultrasonic sensor model.
- Infrared proximity sensor.
- Contact sensor.
- IMU model.
- GPS-like position sensor for suitable outdoor simulations.
- RGB camera model.
- 2D LiDAR model.

Each sensor must have an explicit implementation status and a defined output model.

Control and power components

- Simulated battery.
- Motor driver.
- Controller board representation.
- Signal and power connection points.
- Generic controller component.

These are simulated components. Do not imply electrical circuit-level accuracy unless a separate electrical model is implemented.

6.3 Component manipulation

Users must be able to:

- Add a component.
- Select and deselect components.
- Move and rotate components.
- Attach components to supported connection points.
- Delete components.
- Duplicate components.
- Edit supported properties.
- Inspect the component hierarchy.
- Rename components.
- Save and reload the assembly.

Deleting a connected component must either preserve a valid project state or clearly explain which connections will be removed.

6.4 Robot hierarchy

Every robot must have a structured scene graph.

Example:

Robot

- Chassis
  - Left wheel joint
  - Left wheel
  - Right wheel joint
  - Right wheel
  - Controller
  - Distance sensor

Articulated robots must support parent-child relationships and joint constraints.

The interface must display invalid, disconnected, or unsupported configurations clearly.

6.5 Component property inspector

Depending on component type, expose:

- Position and rotation.
- Dimensions.
- Mass.
- Material.
- Friction.
- Joint type.
- Joint limits.
- Motor speed.
- Motor torque limit.
- Sensor field of view.
- Sensor range.
- Update frequency.
- Connection status.

Unsupported properties must not appear as functional controls.

