9. Differential-Drive Robot

The first fully functional robot should be a two-wheel differential-drive robot.

This robot will validate the complete pipeline before more complex robotic systems are added.

9.1 Required components

- Chassis.
- Left wheel.
- Right wheel.
- Drive joints.
- Optional caster.
- Controller.
- Distance sensor.
- Optional IMU.

9.2 Required behavior

Users must be able to:

- Drive forward.
- Drive backward.
- Rotate left and right.
- Control left and right wheel speeds independently.
- Set wheel radius and wheel separation.
- Observe the robot moving through a simulated environment.
- Detect obstacles.
- Stop the robot when an obstacle is detected.
- Reset the robot to its initial state.

9.3 Kinematics

Implement the standard differential-drive equations.

Let:

- (v_L) = left wheel's linear velocity.
- (v_R) = right wheel's linear velocity.
- (r) = wheel radius.
- (b) = distance between the wheels.

Then:

[
v=\frac{v_R+v_L}{2}
]

[
\omega=\frac{v_R-v_L}{b}
]

Wheel angular velocity is related to linear velocity by:

[
v_L=r\omega_L,\qquad v_R=r\omega_R
]

These calculations must be implemented and tested, not merely displayed in the UI.

The physics engine remains responsible for collisions and physical interactions. Kinematics and physics must be coordinated to avoid contradictory motion models.

