10. Advanced Robotics Features

These features should be developed only after the core simulator is reliable.

10.1 Robotic arms

Support an articulated arm with multiple revolute joints.

Features:

- Joint configuration.
- Joint-angle controls.
- Joint limits.
- Forward kinematics.
- End-effector position.
- Target markers.
- Basic inverse-kinematics solving.
- Collision checks against the environment.
- Animation and trajectory visualization.

Start with a two-link planar arm, then progress to a three-joint arm and eventually a six-axis model.

Do not claim universal inverse-kinematics support. The solver must specify which robot structures it supports.

10.2 Sensor visualization

Distance sensors

Display the measured distance and detection state.

LiDAR

Implement a 2D ray-casting scanner with configurable angular resolution and range.

Render the rays and measured points in the scene. A 3D point-cloud display may be introduced later.

IMU

Provide simulated orientation and acceleration measurements with documented assumptions.

Camera

Introduce a simulated camera feed or rendered sensor view only after the scene rendering and sensor pipeline are working.

10.3 Autonomous navigation

Implement in stages:

1. Known-grid navigation.
2. Grid-based obstacle avoidance.
3. A* path planning.
4. Waypoint following.
5. Obstacle detection and stopping.
6. Dynamic replanning where supported.

The robot must follow a computed path rather than simply teleporting along a displayed line.

10.4 Control systems

Advanced releases may include:

- PID controllers.
- Motor speed control.
- Heading control.
- Error graphs.
- Controller tuning.
- Overshoot and settling-time visualization.
- Repeatable experiments.

All displayed controller outputs must correspond to the actual simulation.

