15. Performance Requirements

RoboLab must target practical performance on real Android hardware.

Initial engineering targets:

- Responsive navigation and project management.
- Smooth camera interaction in the default test scene.
- Target approximately 30 FPS on the defined baseline device.
- Target 60 FPS on capable devices when the scene permits.
- No unbounded memory growth during repeated simulations.
- No application crash when pausing or resuming a simulation.
- No long-running simulation on the UI thread.
- Safe cancellation of simulation and program execution.

These are targets to measure, not guarantees across all Android phones.

15.1 Performance safeguards

- Use low-poly models where appropriate.
- Use level-of-detail strategies when beneficial.
- Limit active rigid bodies.
- Limit sensor rays and update frequencies.
- Pool reusable objects when profiling justifies it.
- Avoid unnecessary allocations in simulation loops.
- Use efficient rendering and physics updates.
- Provide simplified environments for lower-end devices.

A complex six-axis robot with dense collision geometry and high-resolution LiDAR must not be treated as equivalent in cost to a simple wheeled robot.

