18. Testing and Quality Assurance

Testing must cover both software correctness and simulation correctness.

18.1 Unit tests

Test:

- Kinematic equations.
- Unit conversions.
- Project serialization.
- Project migration.
- Component validation.
- Joint-limit validation.
- Program interpreter behavior.
- Controller calculations.
- Path-planning algorithms.

18.2 Integration tests

Test:

- Creating and saving a robot.
- Closing and reopening a project.
- Editing component properties.
- Running and stopping simulations.
- Sensor data flowing into programs.
- Programs controlling robot motion.
- Importing and validating project files.
- Native Android lifecycle and simulator lifecycle interactions.

18.3 Physics validation

Include repeatable test scenarios.

Examples:

- Equal wheel speeds should produce straight-line motion on a symmetric setup.
- Opposite wheel speeds should produce rotation.
- A robot should not pass through a configured solid obstacle.
- Joint-limit violations should be constrained or reported according to the implemented physics model.
- A robot stopped by a program should no longer receive drive commands from that program.

Tests must use tolerances appropriate to the numerical method and physics engine.

18.4 Regression policy

Every phase must preserve completed functionality.

Do not replace real saved projects with sample data during development. Do not reset the project schema without migration. Do not remove working features simply to simplify a new implementation.

