20. Development Roadmap

The following stages define the implementation sequence. Each stage must be independently buildable and testable.

Phase 0 — Technical feasibility

Build a minimal Android application that launches a real 3D scene.

Deliverables:

- Verified Android export.
- Verified Codemagic build.
- Basic 3D camera controls.
- One robot model.
- A small physics test.
- Confirmed app installation on a physical Android phone.

Exit criterion: A reproducible cloud build installs and runs without crashing.

Phase 1 — Native foundation

Implement:

- Application navigation.
- Premium design system.
- Home dashboard.
- Settings.
- Local project database.
- Versioned project serialization.
- Basic error handling.
- Theme selection.

Exit criterion: The application builds, launches, and preserves project metadata across restarts.

Phase 2 — Interactive 3D workshop

Implement:

- 3D viewport.
- Camera controls.
- Component library.
- Component placement and selection.
- Object manipulation.
- Property inspector.
- Robot hierarchy.
- Save and reload.

Exit criterion: A user can build, save, reopen, and modify a basic robot.

Phase 3 — Functional differential-drive simulation

Implement:

- Rigid bodies and collisions.
- Drive joints.
- Wheel motor control.
- Differential-drive kinematics.
- Simulation controls.
- Ground and obstacle environments.
- Distance sensor.
- Telemetry.

Exit criterion: A robot moves according to its actual configuration and responds correctly to obstacles.

Phase 4 — Visual programming

Implement:

- Visual block editor.
- Program validation.
- Interpreter.
- Motor and sensor blocks.
- Conditional logic.
- Loops.
- Execution controls.
- Runtime debugging.

Exit criterion: A user can write a program that controls a robot and observe the resulting behavior.

Phase 5 — Engineering tools

Implement:

- Robotic arm models.
- Supported forward kinematics.
- Basic inverse kinematics.
- Joint visualization.
- 2D LiDAR.
- Path planning.
- Controller graphs.

Exit criterion: Each added subsystem passes dedicated correctness tests and works within defined performance limits.

Phase 6 — Advanced graphics and optimization

Implement:

- Material improvements.
- Lighting and shadow refinements.
- Adaptive graphics.
- Better model assets.
- Performance profiling.
- Memory optimization.
- Landscape workspace improvements.

Exit criterion: The defined benchmark scenes meet the performance targets on the selected test devices.

Phase 7 — Release preparation

Implement:

- Complete regression testing.
- First-launch guidance.
- Recovery and error states.
- Project format validation.
- Release signing configuration.
- Reproducible APK builds.
- Release documentation.

Exit criterion: A clean release build installs, survives lifecycle changes, and preserves user projects.

