23. Critical Implementation Rules for AI Coding

1. Read and understand the existing project structure before editing.
2. Establish a working architecture before generating large amounts of code.
3. Build and test one phase at a time.
4. Never claim that a feature works without implementing and validating its behavior.
5. Never substitute mock data for actual simulation results.
6. Never create decorative controls that imply unsupported functionality.
7. Never remove completed functionality without explicit justification.
8. Preserve user projects through migrations and backward-compatible loading where feasible.
9. Keep rendering, simulation, UI, persistence, and program execution logically separated.
10. Use modular, maintainable code rather than a single enormous source file.
11. Verify Android permissions, lifecycle handling, and Gradle dependencies.
12. Record unresolved technical issues rather than concealing them.
13. Optimize after measuring performance, not through arbitrary feature removal.
14. Do not introduce paid services or API dependencies without explicit approval.
15. Maintain a clear implementation checklist and update it as work progresses.

